  about the  the the power situation because entire  chunks of the country have just been cut  off from the power grid  right uh Cyril I think you wanted first  to ask me about uh what's happening on  the Russian side and that's uh basically  summarized in the statements by Sergey  sorovicin the new Commander in Chief for  the Russian special operation in eastern  Ukraine Eastern Southern Ukraine he  basically acknowledged for the first  time that Russia is in a difficult  situation I mean the Russian army there  and he said the evacuation of civilians  must happen as soon as possible because  Russia wants to make sure those citizens  are protected and Russia has the ability  to protect them they were provided safe  and free transfer and crossing and  accommodation on the southern side of  the river the junipro river and also  they were urged to even go into Russia  itself if they want the Russians saying  that this is a voluntary transfer if  they don't want to go they they can stay  but they have to take the risks of  staying where they are right now they  are talking about a new push and new  plans by the Ukrainian Army to uh to  attack her son and particularly the  infrastructure  he talked about the dam that you have  just mentioned a little earlier or  Charles has just mentioned that Dam has  been compromised and it might release  what its Hydro power Dam that might  release water and that could cause  floodings that will further jeopardize  the lives of those civilians it's a  tough situation remember that usually in  Russia it's the Minister of Defense the  Minister of Defense who gives statements  of this type or the the the the  commander of chief of all the armored  forces but this man has just been  appointed a couple weeks ago and this is  a precedent that he is giving such a  huge uh statements and remarks about  what's happening and particularly about  the difficulties Russians are facing but  I also said there will be tough choices  to make and tough decisions to take  during the next few days and  understandably you know he's known his  history in Syria he led the operation  there the destruction of Aleppo and so  on and we have seen this increase during  the last few days of those suicide  drones across Ukraine very much a  reminder of of what could happen what  the Russians might do and also a  reminisce reminiscent of what happened  in Syria  the remember the uh the uh the the the  the the the barrel bombs in Syria and so  on so I mean he's he's certainly from  his words between the lines he's  planning something something big but at  first he wants the civilians to be moved  away from that area before the Russians  take a tougher and more drastic measures  there all right Mohammed Val thank you  very much for your reporting from Moscow  thank you for that mistake at the top uh  now let us go to Muhammad James he is  live for us in Kiev and so Muhammad I  was planning to ask you that's what I  was telling Mohammed Val earlier ask you  about the power situation how we're  doing there but I also can't help but  notice that you've put the best the vest  and the helmet back on tell us what's  been going on where you are  well that's right Cyril just in the past  15 minutes the air raid sirens started  ringing out across the city of Kiev it's  the first air alert that we've had today  over the course of the past few days  many times throughout the early morning  hours uh throughout the the early  afternoon and sometimes even in the  evening the air raid sirens have rung  out because there were two consecutive  days of air strikes here you had drone  strikes on Monday you had missile  strikes that were hitting around the  area uh yesterday on on Tuesday we have  not had any reports as of yet of any  missiles targeting any specific areas or  any drones being spotted above and we've  not had any reports yet of the air  defense systems of Ukraine shooting down  any incoming objects that all being said  because of the strikes that I was just  telling you about that happened uh first  by the Kamikaze drones on Monday and  then by the missiles yesterday there  have been many power infrastructure  sites across the country and even close  to the Capitol that that have been hit  these past 48 hours because of that  there has been a great deal of concern  being expressed from Ukrainian President  Vladimir zielinski and many other  Ukrainian officials as to the fact that  so many critical infrastructure sites  have been damaged they're saying that at  least 30 percent of the country's power  stations have been hit since October  10th and because of that they are urging  citizens to start to try to conserve on  their power usage they're also saying  that there could be power blackouts  happening in the days and weeks to come  this is a big concern right now because  temperatures are dropping precipitously  as winter approaches now one of the  areas hit close to where we are  yesterday is the desnyansky district  that's east of the capital it's about 30  minutes away from where we are right now  in the center of Kiev we spent several  hours there yesterday when we were there  speaking to residents of an apartment  complex they were without water they  were without power it's the second time  it's happened to them in the last two  weeks and they were really worried about  what this bodes for them for the weeks  and months ahead since we spoke to them  we have been told that power in that  area and utilities have all come back  online and that's really the pattern  that we've been seeing a lot in the last  week in the areas that we visited when  there are strikes when power is offline  it doesn't seem to take too much time  before power is restored in those areas  that being said though there are still  areas of the country where power is out  and the concern right now is that there  will be more areas of the country where  power is out in the coming days weeks  and months if there are continued  Russian strikes this is what Ukrainian  officials have been saying zero all  right Muhammad reporting from Keith  thank you very much

